// Real case: Vertex/CRISPR — Casgevy (exagamglogene autotemcel)
// FDA-approved December 2023 for sickle cell disease and transfusion-dependent beta-thalassemia.
//
// Mechanism: CRISPR-Cas9 disruption of the BCL11A erythroid-specific enhancer
// at the +58 region, reactivating fetal hemoglobin (HbF) production.
// Guide targets the GATA1 binding site in the BCL11A enhancer.
//
// Published outcome (Frangoul et al., NEJM 2021, doi:10.1056/NEJMoa2031054):
//   - Ex vivo editing of patient CD34+ HSCs, then autologous transplant
//   - Sustained HbF elevation (>40%) in treated patients
//   - Transfusion independence achieved in most patients
//   - This is enhancer disruption, not viral reservoir elimination
//
// Known ground truth: eliminates_reservoir should be REFUSED.
// This is not a reservoir elimination — it's enhancer disruption to
// reactivate a compensatory gene. The BCL11A gene itself is not excised,
// only its erythroid enhancer is disrupted. The "excise" framing is
// technically correct (a segment is cut) but the therapeutic goal is
// not reservoir elimination.

target BCL11AEnhancer {
  organism human
  tolerance 0
  signatures TCAAGGTCCTAAGCGAT
  in [ hematopoietic.stem.cell ] @measured(human)
}

excise BCL11AEnhancer
